Factless fact tables — events and coverage.

Every fact table so far has held measures. The last type has none. A factless fact table is all foreign keys and no numeric measures. It records that an event, or a relationship, happened — and you get your metric by counting the rows.

It sounds like a contradiction — a fact with no facts — but it's common and genuinely useful. The row's existence is the fact. Student attendance is the textbook case: one row per student, per class, per day means was present. How many attended? Count star. There are no amounts to sum — just keys — and the count of rows is the answer.

There are two flavours. The first is event tracking: recording that something occurred — an attendance, a login, a customer-service contact, a product movement. Count the rows, grouped by dimension, and you have your metric. The second is coverage: recording what was possible, so you can analyse what didn't happen. Which products were on promotion on which day — whether or not they actually sold. On its own, that's just a coverage fact. Its power comes from comparison: join it against FACT SALES and find the promoted products that never sold — the promotions that missed. A sales fact can only tell you what happened; a coverage fact lets you ask about the negative space.

So where does it earn its place? Counting events — attendance, enrolments, interactions, clicks. Analysing absence — no-shows, unsold promotions, gaps in coverage. And audit and compliance — proving a required event did, or did not, occur. A factless fact is keys with no measures: its metric is count star, and its superpower is letting you analyse what didn't happen. And that closes out the fact table — next, we turn to the dimensions that give all these facts their context.
